@page "/review"
@inject IDraftValidationService DraftValidationService
@inject IRecapService RecapService
@inject IDraftStateService DraftStateService
@inject ICoherenceService CoherenceService
@inject ICoherenceApplyService CoherenceApplyService

@*
    The first thing in Alembic that is useful on its own. Two panels, in this order
    on purpose: what would stop this domain, then what its model actually reads. Showing a
    beautifully composed prompt for a configuration that would not start is a way of lying to the
    client with something that looks like evidence.
*@

<div class="alembic-page">

    <FinalizationRail At="0" />

    @if (DraftStateService.Current is not { } draft)
    {
        <div class="panel">
            <h2>Nothing to review</h2>
            <p>Import an <code>agents.json</code> first.</p>
            <p><a href="import">Go to import &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @*
            One panel, not two: a deterministic check and a relational one are the same job seen at
            two depths, not two different jobs. The first is decidable by reading the Draft alone —
            no model is asked, and none would help. The second needs the whole domain read at once,
            because whether two intents collide or two agents claim the same ground is invisible one
            agent at a time, which is exactly how the interview writes them. Together they are what
            "would stop this domain, or should give you pause" means in full.
        *@
        <div class="panel">
            <h2>Domain Validation</h2>
            <p class="muted">What would stop this domain, and what only reading it whole reveals.</p>

            <details class="recap-section">
                <summary>Deterministic checks</summary>
                <p class="muted">What Morgana would refuse to start on &mdash; reads off the Draft alone, no model involved.</p>
                @if (findings.Count == 0)
                {
                    <p class="all-clear">&check; Nothing to report. Every deterministic check passes.</p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <p class="muted">
                        @findings.Count(f => f.Severity == FindingSeverity.Error) errors,
                        @findings.Count(f => f.Severity == FindingSeverity.Warning) warnings.
                        Each one restates a rule Morgana enforces later and more expensively.
                    </p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th></th><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (ValidationFinding finding in findings)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td class="@(finding.Severity == FindingSeverity.Error ? "severity-error" : "severity-warning")">
                                        @(finding.Severity == FindingSeverity.Error ? "error" : "warning")
                                    </td>
                                    <td><code>@finding.Where</code></td>
                                    <td>
                                        @finding.Message
                                        <div class="because">@finding.Because</div>
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                }
            </details>

            <details class="recap-section">
                <summary>Coherence</summary>
            <p class="muted">
                What only reading every agent at once reveals. Ask it for what you actually want to
                know: each class below is a separate question about your domain, and the pass is
                composed of the ones you tick and nothing else.
            </p>

            @*
                Ticked one at a time rather than run whole, and the reason is not the token bill. A
                report answering six questions when the client asked one is a table to sift, and a
                table to sift is a table nobody finishes; worse, the class they actually came about
                arrives ranked beneath five they did not. So the choice is theirs, nothing is ticked
                for them, and the pass cannot run until they have said what they are looking for.
            *@
            <div class="aspects">
                @foreach (CoherenceAspect aspect in CoherenceService.Aspects)
                {
                    string id = aspect.Id;
                    <label class="switch aspect">
                        <input type="checkbox" checked="@asked.Contains(id)" @onchange="e => ToggleAspect(id, e)" />
                        <span>
                            @aspect.Label
                            <span class="field-note">@aspect.Summary</span>
                        </span>
                    </label>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                Running the pass itself is optional and cheap to try; it is "Apply" that writes directly
                into the domain the interview built, possibly across more than one agent, and the whole
                loop is iterative with no guarantee it ends on its own...
            *@
            <div class="power-notice">
                <svg class="sigil" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                     stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="M13 2 5 14h6l-1 8 9-13h-6z" />
                </svg>
                <div>
                    <strong>100% AI-driven, applying its suggestions will modify what the interview built.</strong>
                    Running it is optional &mdash; good practice, to see what it finds. Applying is
                    where it acts: prose or tools, sometimes more than one agent, iterative, and it
                    can cost more than one round.
                </div>
            </div>

            @if (coherence is null)
            {
                <button class="primary" @onclick="ReviewAsync" disabled="@(isReviewing || draft.Agents.Count < 2 || asked.Count == 0)">
                    @(isReviewing ? "Reading the domain…" : Asking)
                </button>

                @if (draft.Agents.Count < 2)
                {
                    <p class="muted">
                        One agent, so there is nothing to relate: this pass needs two.
                    </p>
                }
                else if (asked.Count == 0)
                {
                    <p class="muted">
                        Nothing ticked yet, so there is no question to ask. Pick what you want looked at.
                    </p>
                }
            }
            else if (coherence.Error is not null)
            {
                <p class="severity-warning">@coherence.Error</p>
                <button class="agent-pick" @onclick="ReviewAsync" disabled="@isReviewing">Try again</button>
            }
            else if (coherence.Findings.Count == 0)
            {
                @* Nothing in the domain can have changed since this same pass just read it clean, so
                   re-running THE SAME question would spend another Performance call to hear the same
                   answer. A different question is another matter entirely, and that is exactly what
                   ticking a class the last run did not carry is — so the button comes back for that
                   and for nothing else. *@
                <p class="all-clear">&check; Nothing to report on @Ran.</p>

                @if (AskedSomethingElse)
                {
                    <button class="primary" @onclick="ReviewAsync" disabled="@isReviewing">@Asking</button>
                }
            }
            else
            {
                @* One card per finding, not a table row: the three things a client needs to read —
                   what is wrong, what it costs, what to do — are paragraphs of prose, and a row that
                   stacks all three in one cell against a button floating level with the first of
                   them tells nobody which part the button acts on. The card gives each its own
                   labelled place and puts "Apply" inside the fix it applies.

                   No bulk apply: the notice above asks for care applying one at a time, and a button
                   that fires every suggestion unread is the opposite of that — especially once a fix
                   can touch more than one agent on its own. *@
                <div class="coherence-findings">
                    @for (int i = 0; i < coherence.Findings.Count; i++)
                    {
                        CoherenceFinding finding = coherence.Findings[i];
                        int index = i;
                        bool solved = applyResults.TryGetValue(index, out CoherenceApplyResult? applied) && applied.Applied;

                        <article class="finding @(solved ? "finding-solved" : null)">
                            <header class="finding-head">
                                <span class="finding-kind">@AspectLabel(finding.Kind)</span>
                                <code class="finding-where">@finding.Where</code>
                                @if (solved)
                                {
                                    <span class="badge-solved">&check; Solved</span>
                                }
                            </header>

                            @if (RelatedFix(finding) is { } related)
                            {
                                @* Shares an agent with something just fixed: read at a glance this
                                   looks like the same problem back, and it is not — it is what the
                                   fix touched, seen from a different angle. Say so before the client
                                   has to work that out from the Kind alone. *@
                                <p class="consequence-of">
                                    &darr; surfaced by the &ldquo;@AspectLabel(related.Finding.Kind)&rdquo; fix on <code>@related.Finding.Where</code>
                                </p>
                            }

                            <p class="finding-label">What is wrong</p>
                            <p class="finding-text">@finding.What</p>

                            <p class="finding-label">What it costs at runtime</p>
                            <p class="finding-text">@finding.Why</p>

                            <div class="finding-fix">
                                <div class="finding-fix-body">
                                    <p class="finding-label">Suggested fix</p>
                                    <p class="finding-text">@finding.Fix</p>
                                    @if (applyResults.TryGetValue(index, out CoherenceApplyResult? result))
                                    {
                                        <p class="finding-outcome @(result.Applied ? null : "finding-outcome-failed")">@result.Summary</p>
                                    }
                                </div>
                                @if (!solved)
                                {
                                    <div class="finding-fix-act">
                                        @if (applyingIndex == index)
                                        {
                                            @* Not a button: a control the client cannot press is a control that
                                               should not look pressable, and a disabled button with the same
                                               chrome as an active one still invites the click it will refuse. *@
                                            <span class="applying">Applying&hellip;</span>
                                        }
                                        else
                                        {
                                            <button class="agent-pick" @onclick="() => ApplyAsync(index, finding)" disabled="@applyingIndex.HasValue">
                                                Apply suggestion
                                            </button>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        </article>
                    }
                </div>

                @* Only once something has actually changed: re-running against a domain nobody
                   touched since the last pass would hand the model the exact same cards and spend
                   a call to hear the exact same findings back. *@
                @if (AnyApplied || AskedSomethingElse)
                {
                    @if (coherenceRuns >= ChaseWarningThreshold)
                    {
                        @* Nothing here stops the client — it advises, never blocks, same as every
                           finding it reports — but a fix's own ripple can surface a fresh finding
                           every time, and nothing about this loop guarantees it ever reaches zero.
                           Past a few rounds the honest thing is to say so plainly, not to keep
                           lighting the button as if one more press were the one that finishes it. *@
                        <p class="chase-warning">
                            @coherenceRuns passes this sitting, each one landing another change on
                            the domain the interview built. Weigh what is left yourself before
                            running it again &mdash; the loop is not going to tell you when to stop.
                        </p>
                        <button class="agent-pick" @onclick="ReviewAsync" disabled="@(isReviewing || applyingIndex.HasValue)">Run it again anyway</button>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <button class="primary" @onclick="ReviewAsync" disabled="@(isReviewing || applyingIndex.HasValue)">@(AskedSomethingElse ? Asking : "Run it again")</button>
                    }
                }
            }
            </details>
        </div>

        <div class="panel">
            <h2>Composed prompts</h2>
            <p>
                @foreach (AgentDraft agent in draft.Agents)
                {
                    <button class="agent-pick @(selectedAgentId == agent.ID ? "picked" : "")"
                            @onclick="() => SelectAsync(agent)">@agent.ID</button>
                }
            </p>

            @if (recap is not null)
            {
                @* Two fenced blocks, split for the same reason they are fenced at all: they are
                   read at two different moments of standing (Morgana's, then this agent's), and a
                   client weighing one prose against the other should never have to find the seam
                   by scrolling. The split point is the domain fence ComposeAgentInstructionsAsync
                   always writes — see SplitSystemPrompt. *@
                (string frameworkPrompt, string agentPrompt) = SplitSystemPrompt(recap.SystemPrompt);

                <div class="nested-recap">
                    <details class="recap-section">
                        <summary>System prompt</summary>
                        <p class="muted">
                            Morgana's own layer, read once before anything else and binding on every
                            agent of hers &mdash; Alembic did not write a word of this and cannot: it
                            is embedded in the Morgana.AI this Alembic was built against, and
                            customising it means forking the framework, not editing this domain.
                        </p>
                        <pre class="prompt">@frameworkPrompt</pre>
                    </details>

                    <details class="recap-section">
                        <summary>Agent prompt</summary>
                        <p class="muted">
                            This is Alembic's own work: what this agent's interview wrote, subordinate
                            to the framework above on every point. If something here reads wrong, this
                            is the half you can still change &mdash; go back into the interview.
                        </p>
                        <pre class="prompt">@agentPrompt</pre>
                    </details>

                    @if (recap.Tools.Count > 0)
                    {
                        <details class="recap-section">
                        <summary>Toolkit</summary>
                        @foreach (ToolRecap tool in recap.Tools)
                        {
                            <div class="tool-card">
                                <h4><code>@tool.Name</code></h4>
                                <p class="tool-desc">@tool.Description</p>
                                @if (tool.Parameters.Count > 0)
                                {
                                    <table>
                                        <thead>
                                            <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Scope</th><th>Required</th><th>Description, as the schema carries it</th></tr>
                                        </thead>
                                        <tbody>
                                            @foreach (ParameterRecap parameter in tool.Parameters)
                                            {
                                                <tr>
                                                    <td><code>@parameter.Name</code></td>
                                                    <td>@(parameter.Scope ?? "—")</td>
                                                    <td>@(parameter.Required ? "yes" : "no")</td>
                                                    <td class="description">
                                                        @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(parameter.Description)
                                                            ? "(emitted bare — no description reaches the model)"
                                                            : parameter.Description)
                                                    </td>
                                                </tr>
                                            }
                                        </tbody>
                                    </table>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                        </details>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @*
        Where the domain goes next. A review that ends in a dead end reads as a verdict on one
        finished agent; the two things a client does after reading it are add the next agent and
        morganize the whole domain, so both are here rather than behind the front door.
    *@
    @if (DraftStateService.Current is not null)
    {
        <div class="gateways">
            <a class="gateway primary-gateway" href="interview">
                <h2>Distil a new agent</h2>
                <span class="go">Begin &rarr;</span>
            </a>

            <a class="gateway" href="morganize">
                <h2>Morganize it</h2>
                <span class="go">Package &rarr;</span>
            </a>
        </div>
    }

    <Back />

</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Deterministic findings for the current Draft, refreshed by <see cref="OnInitialized"/> and after every applied fix.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<ValidationFinding> findings = [];

    /// <summary><c>null</c> until the client runs the coherence pass at least once this visit.</summary>
    private CoherenceReport? coherence;

    /// <summary>Whether the coherence pass is in flight, so the button reads "Reading the domain…" and cannot be pressed twice.</summary>
    private bool isReviewing;

    /// <summary>
    /// The classes of defect the client has ticked, by <see cref="CoherenceAspect.Id"/>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Empty on arrival, deliberately: this pass is a question about their domain and there is no
    /// question they are known to have. Ticking every box for them would put the old whole-pass
    /// behaviour back under a control that says it is a choice.
    /// </remarks>
    private readonly HashSet<string> asked = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>
    /// What the last run actually asked, so a re-run can tell a new question from the same one.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly HashSet<string> ran = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    /// <summary>The composed prompt for <see cref="selectedAgentId"/>, or <c>null</c> when no agent is picked.</summary>
    private AgentRecap? recap;

    /// <summary>Which agent's prompt is currently shown; <c>null</c> means none is.</summary>
    private string? selectedAgentId;

    /// <summary>The coherence finding currently being applied, so only one Apply can run at a time.</summary>
    private int? applyingIndex;

    /// <summary>The outcome of every applied fix this sitting, keyed by the finding's position in <see cref="coherence"/>.</summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<int, CoherenceApplyResult> applyResults = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Every finding successfully applied this sitting, across every coherence run — not cleared
    /// alongside <see cref="applyResults"/>, because carrying it forward is the whole point.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly List<ResolvedCoherenceFinding> resolvedThisSitting = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether at least one coherence finding has actually landed on the Draft since this pass last
    /// ran — the one condition under which running it again could tell the client anything new.
    /// </summary>
    private bool AnyApplied => applyResults.Values.Any(r => r.Applied);

    /// <summary>
    /// How many times the coherence pass has run this sitting. Nothing here guarantees it ever
    /// reaches zero findings — a fix's own ripple can surface a fresh one every round — so this is
    /// what lets the client see the loop they are in rather than infer it from memory.
    /// </summary>
    private int coherenceRuns;

    /// <summary>
    /// Past this many rounds, "Run it again" stops being the obvious next click and starts asking
    /// for a second thought instead. Two, not three: one fix revealing one more finding is already
    /// the cherries-out-of-the-bowl pattern this exists to interrupt, not an exception to wait out.
    /// </summary>
    private const int ChaseWarningThreshold = 2;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the button offers to do, counted in the client's own ticks rather than named as a pass.
    /// </summary>
    private string Asking =>
        asked.Count == 1 ? "Ask about this one thing" : $"Ask about these {asked.Count} things";

    /// <summary>
    /// The finding's class in the same words its checkbox carried, so a card names the thing the
    /// client ticked rather than the raw <c>kind</c> slug the model answered with.
    /// </summary>
    private string AspectLabel(string kind) =>
        CoherenceService.Aspects.FirstOrDefault(a => a.Id == kind)?.Label ?? kind;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the last run was asked, in the labels the client ticked.
    /// </summary>
    private string Ran =>
        string.Join(", ", CoherenceService.Aspects.Where(a => ran.Contains(a.Id)).Select(a => a.Label.ToLowerInvariant()));

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the ticks now differ from the ones the last run carried — the one thing that makes
    /// another call worth making over a domain nobody has touched since.
    /// </summary>
    private bool AskedSomethingElse => asked.Count > 0 && !asked.SetEquals(ran);

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes a class of defect in or out of the next run. Nothing already reported is disturbed:
    /// what is on screen is the answer to the question that was asked, and it stays true of it.
    /// </summary>
    private void ToggleAspect(string aspectId, ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Value is true)
            asked.Add(aspectId);
        else
            asked.Remove(aspectId);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Runs the deterministic checks against whatever Draft is already in the circuit — the
    /// coherence pass is never run here, since it costs a model call and the client has not asked
    /// for it yet.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The composed-prompts panel opens on the domain's first agent rather than empty, so the
    /// evidence a client came here to read is already on screen instead of behind a first click —
    /// <see cref="SelectAsync"/> does the one composition this costs, same as picking it by hand
    /// would.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        if (DraftStateService.Current is not { } draft)
            return;

        findings = DraftValidationService.Validate(draft);

        if (draft.Agents.FirstOrDefault() is { } first)
            await SelectAsync(first);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Runs (or re-runs) the coherence pass and clears any per-finding apply results from the run
    /// before it — they described findings this fresh read may not even repeat.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ReviewAsync()
    {
        if (DraftStateService.Current is not { } draft)
            return;

        isReviewing = true;
        coherenceRuns++;
        applyResults.Clear();
        try { coherence = await CoherenceService.ReviewAsync(draft, [.. asked], resolvedThisSitting); }
        finally { isReviewing = false; }

        // What was actually asked, recorded after the call rather than before it: the button that
        // offers another run reads the difference between this and the ticks standing now.
        ran.Clear();
        ran.UnionWith(asked);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The literal <c>ConfigurationPromptComposerService</c> writes to open the domain layer — see
    /// its <c>DomainLayerHeader</c>. Duplicated here rather than exposed by the framework because
    /// this is a display-only seam: nothing downstream of the model ever needs the two layers apart,
    /// only this panel does, and the marker is fixed in code on both sides of the reference (the
    /// framework's own remarks call the fences "glue... not domain-tunable prose", so it does not
    /// move without the framework's prompt shape moving with it).
    /// </summary>
    private const string DomainLayerMarker = "======== DOMAIN AGENT";

    /// <summary>
    /// Splits a composed system prompt at the domain fence into Morgana's own layer and this
    /// agent's, so the panel can show each on its own — read at two different moments of the turn,
    /// as explained where they are rendered.
    /// </summary>
    /// <returns>
    /// The framework half and the agent half; if the marker is not found (a composer from a build
    /// this page was never tested against), everything falls back into the framework half rather
    /// than silently dropping text — the client still sees the whole prompt, just not split.
    /// </returns>
    private static (string Framework, string Agent) SplitSystemPrompt(string systemPrompt)
    {
        int index = systemPrompt.IndexOf(DomainLayerMarker, StringComparison.Ordinal);
        return index < 0
            ? (systemPrompt, string.Empty)
            : (systemPrompt[..index].TrimEnd(), systemPrompt[index..].TrimEnd());
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Picks an agent's recap, or lets it go if it is already the one showing.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A picker with no way back to "nothing picked" is not a toggle, it is a one-way door: the tag
    /// stays lit and the only way to stop reading this agent's prompt is to read another's instead.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task SelectAsync(AgentDraft agent)
    {
        if (selectedAgentId == agent.ID)
        {
            selectedAgentId = null;
            recap = null;
            return;
        }

        selectedAgentId = agent.ID;
        recap = await RecapService.ComposeAsync(agent, IsConsultedByOthers(agent));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// True when some OTHER agent of the draft declares it may consult this one — the half of the
    /// consultation relation an agent cannot see from its own <c>Consults</c> list.
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsConsultedByOthers(AgentDraft agent)
        => DraftStateService.Current?.Agents.Any(other =>
               other.ID != agent.ID &&
               other.Code.Consults.Contains(agent.ID, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) ?? false;

    /// <summary>
    /// Applies one coherence finding and refreshes both the deterministic findings and the open
    /// recap, since a fix can touch more than one agent.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ApplyAsync(int index, CoherenceFinding finding)
    {
        if (DraftStateService.Current is not { } draft || applyingIndex is not null)
            return;

        applyingIndex = index;

        try { await ApplyOneAsync(draft, index, finding); }
        finally { applyingIndex = null; }

        await RefreshAfterApplyAsync(draft);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The already-applied fix this finding most plausibly follows from, if its Where shares ground
    /// with one — the case that reads, at a glance, like the same problem come back.
    /// </summary>
    private ResolvedCoherenceFinding? RelatedFix(CoherenceFinding finding)
    {
        HashSet<string> words = GroundWords(finding.Where);
        return resolvedThisSitting.FirstOrDefault(r => words.Overlaps(GroundWords(r.Finding.Where)));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A pattern compiled once rather than re-parsed on every <see cref="GroundWords"/> call.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly Regex NonWordRun = new Regex("[^a-z0-9]+", RegexOptions.Compiled);

    /// <summary>
    /// The words of four letters or more in a Where string, lowercased — the vocabulary
    /// <see cref="RelatedFix"/> compares two findings' Where strings by, since a comma-separated
    /// list of agent and intent names sharing one almost never happens by coincidence.
    /// </summary>
    private static HashSet<string> GroundWords(string where) =>
        [.. NonWordRun.Split(where.ToLowerInvariant()).Where(w => w.Length > 3)];

    private async Task ApplyOneAsync(DomainDraft draft, int index, CoherenceFinding finding)
    {
        CoherenceApplyResult result = await CoherenceApplyService.ApplyAsync(draft, finding);
        applyResults[index] = result;

        // Carried forward across every coherence run this sitting, never cleared with applyResults:
        // its whole job is to be there the next time ReviewAsync runs, so that run can tell a finding
        // it already dealt with apart from one still genuinely present.
        if (result.Applied)
            resolvedThisSitting.Add(new ResolvedCoherenceFinding(finding, result.Summary));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Re-reads the Draft after one or more fixes have landed on it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Run even after a declared failure: the applier's own tool calls can land on the Draft before
    /// it stops without confirming, so a stale findings list or a stale recap would show the client
    /// less than what may already have changed.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task RefreshAfterApplyAsync(DomainDraft draft)
    {
        findings = DraftValidationService.Validate(draft);
        DraftStateService.Set(draft);

        if (selectedAgentId is not null && draft.Agents.FirstOrDefault(a => a.ID == selectedAgentId) is { } selected)
            recap = await RecapService.ComposeAsync(selected, IsConsultedByOthers(selected));
    }
}
